## Amendment, declared before any arm landed

Batch 1 is in flight and no arm has produced output yet. What follows changes **how a narrow
result is labelled**, never whether the candidate lands, and it is written now for that reason.

`harness/README.md` line 140 reads: *"At N=3 a real mechanism shows as clean separation (0/3 vs
3/3); anything narrower is noise and should be treated as a null."* **That sentence assumes the
control scores zero.** Where the control has a real non-zero base rate, as condition A does here
(14.12's G side fired 1/3 without any protocol change), the rule silently converts *unresolvable
at this N* into *null*. In this repo a null is not a shrug: Decision record and the DRT reply
both state it plainly — **a null means the rule was tested properly and had no effect, so the
rule is deleted; an inconclusive means the test was invalid, so nothing changes and we say so.**
A 3/3-against-1/3 outcome is neither. It is under-powered, and filing it as a null would record
a measurement this round did not make.

The repo's own practice already disagrees with its README. 14.11 was refused on exactly this
arithmetic, against a control rate of 2 in 6, and was called **uninterpretable** rather than null
(`05`, 2026-11-03). The README never absorbed that.

**So, fixed now:**
- **B 3/3 on (i) against A 0/3** → lands, as pre-registered. Unchanged.
- **Anything narrower** → the generated-code-map line closes, as pre-registered. Unchanged.
- **The label, when A is non-zero:** `inconclusive — under-powered against a non-zero control`,
  not `null`. The candidate still does not land and `05` still records that it did not. What
  changes is that the record will not claim the clause was shown ineffective when what was shown
  is that three arms a side cannot see the difference.

This amendment cannot save the candidate and is not meant to. The stopping rule holds.

Next intended action: await batch 1, verify both arms completed, then launch batch 2.

